    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for my clinical rotations. Can you give me a rundown on how melanoma usually presents in a standard patient? Like, what are the primary visual cues doctors look for during a routine exam?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Melanoma often presents as a new or changing mole. Key visual cues include asymmetry, irregular borders, multiple colors, large diameter, and evolving features like size, shape, or color. Dilation of blood vessels on or near the mole and the presence of ulceration are also telling signs. Regular skin examinations during clinical rotations are crucial for early detection and successful treatment of melanoma.",
